2026-09-27T03:28:10.3809660Z Disclaimer: there's no guarantee of timely updates in case of security problems or other issues. If you want to use them in a security-or-otherwise-critical environment (say, on a production server), you do so at your own risk.
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3810669Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3810769Z Update Note
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3810989Z ===========
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3811308Z Please use this repository instead of ppa:fkrull/deadsnakes.
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3811615Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3811716Z Reporting Issues
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3811943Z ================
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3812173Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3812331Z Issues can be reported in the master issue tracker at:
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3812729Z https://github.com/deadsnakes/issues/issues
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3812959Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3813088Z Supported Ubuntu and Python Versions
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3813391Z ====================================
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3813570Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3813725Z - Ubuntu 22.04 (jammy) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.10)
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3814233Z - Ubuntu 24.04 (noble) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.12)
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3814622Z - Ubuntu 26.04 (resolute) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.14)
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3815377Z - Note: Python 3.10 (jammy), Python3.12 (noble), Python3.14 (resolute) are not provided by deadsnakes as upstream ubuntu provides those packages.
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3815966Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3816085Z Why some packages aren't built:
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3816550Z - Note: for 22.04+, older python versions requre libssl<3 so they are not currently built
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3817133Z - If you need these, reach out to asottile to set up a private ppa
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3817426Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3817759Z The packages may also work on other versions of Ubuntu or Debian, but that is not tested or supported.
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3818188Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3818286Z Packages
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3818496Z ========
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3818620Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3820159Z The packages provided here are loosely based on the debian upstream packages with some modifications to make them more usable as non-default pythons and on ubuntu.  As such, the packages follow debian's patterns and often do not include a full python distribution with just `apt install python#.#`.  Here is a list of packages that may be useful along with the default install:
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3821728Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3822005Z - `python#.#-dev`: includes development headers for building C extensions
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3822553Z - `python#.#-venv`: provides the standard library `venv` module
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3823074Z - `python#.#-distutils`: provides the standard library `distutils` module
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3823746Z - `python#.#-lib2to3`: provides the `2to3-#.#` utility as well as the standard library `lib2to3` module
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3824639Z - `python#.#-gdbm`: provides the standard library `dbm.gnu` module
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3825149Z - `python#.#-tk`: provides the standard library `tkinter` module
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3825464Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3825936Z Third-Party Python Modules
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3895072Z ==========================
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3895276Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3896532Z Python modules in the official Ubuntu repositories are packaged to work with the Python interpreters from the official repositories. Accordingly, they generally won't work with the Python interpreters from this PPA. As an exception, pure-Python modules for Python 3 will work, but any compiled extension modules won't.
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3898108Z 
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3898823Z To install 3rd-party Python modules, you should use the common Python packaging tools.  For an introduction into the Python packaging ecosystem and its tools, refer to the Python Packaging User Guide:
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3899734Z https://packaging.python.org/installing/
2026-09-27T03:28:10.3899985Z 
